%description:

A defect in the shipped BLER curves, recorded rather than fixed: block error
rate is not monotonically non-increasing in SINR.

Physically it has to be. A modulation that fails a given fraction of the time
at some SINR cannot fail more often when the signal gets stronger, and the
CQI search in LteFeedbackComputationRealistic::getCqi -- which walks the
curves looking for the one whose BLER sits closest to the target -- is only
well defined if they are ordered.

BLERvsSINR_15CQI_TU.h is a table of measured points rather than a formula, so
these look like measurement noise or transcription slips that were never
filtered out. Three adjacent pairs in BLER_15_CQI_TU go the wrong way:

    CQI  1   SINR -1.5 -> -0.5    BLER 0     -> 0.004
    CQI  4   SINR  4.0 ->  5.0    BLER 0.008 -> 0.02
    CQI  5   SINR -3.0 -> -2.0    BLER 0.988 -> 0.996

The CQI 1 pair is the odd one: the curve reaches exactly zero at -1.5 dB and
then comes back up, which also means "error-free" is reported one point before
the curve actually ends.

The effect on a simulation is small -- the deviations are a fraction of a
percent, and only the CQI 4 pair sits anywhere near a plausible target BLER --
so this is filed as a data-quality problem, not a correctness emergency.
Fixing it means deciding what the curves ought to be, which is a modelling
question and not a refactoring one; until then, asserting it here keeps the
fact visible and stops anyone concluding the curves are ordered.

When this is fixed, drop the %expected-failure line and move the check into
BlerCqiRoundTrip.test, next to the other properties of the curves.

%expected-failure: the shipped BLER curves are non-monotonic at three points

%includes:
#include "SchedulerTestUtil.h"
#include "simu5g/common/blerCurves/PhyPisaData.h"

%global:
using namespace simu5g;
using namespace simu5g::unittest;

%activity:
Checker c;
PhyPisaData pisa;

int offenders = 0;
for (int cqi = 1; cqi <= 15; cqi++)
    for (int s = -30; s < 50; s++)
        if (pisa.getBler(cqi, s + 1) > pisa.getBler(cqi, s))
            offenders++;

c.check("BLER is non-increasing in SINR, for every CQI", offenders == 0,
        "sampled 1 dB steps where BLER rises: " + std::to_string(offenders));

c.summary();

%contains: stdout
modelchecks=1 modelfailures=0

%not-contains: stdout
FAILED
